            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attack hatch (can be sealed with a removable caulking such as Draft Attack)

 

** The bathroom fan &amp; ceiling light fixture in the main floor bathroom



** The many ceiling potlights that have been installed on the main floor



** Where the wall meets the ceiling by the stairs to the basement



** Along the top trim of the windows, especially the large window in living room



** The current chimney that co-vents the furnace &amp; hot water tank



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. I also want to point out that your house also appears to have a lack of mechanical ventilation in the bathrooms &amp; kitchen, and you should look at my section on ventilation for further information.

</airSeal>
                <ceilings>

There are 2 attic spaces in this house - one above the main house and another above the addition for the entrance. I could see into the one for the main house and there was only the original R20 insulation from the 70's. I have modeled the opportunity to increase the attic insulation in the original house up to a minimum R50.



Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R20 to R50, this is estimated to save $ 355 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11.5 GJ, and qualify for a $ 721 rebate through the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</ceilings>
                <VentilationSystem>

See my comments in the Air Sealing &amp; Additional Comments sections of this report.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 121 litre oil hot water tank that co-vents with the oil furnace up a chimney. In conjunction with a furnace upgrade (please see the section of my report on this), I have modeled replacing your oil hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 302 per year in energy cost, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 82.1% efficient oil furnace that co-vents up a chimney with the oil hot water tank. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. If both the furnace &amp; hot water are upgraded to high efficiency units vented out through the side wall header space of the basement, than the chimney will no longer be required (a key location of air leakage heat loss).



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane  furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 1028 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has 2 toilets and the one in the basement is an older 13 litre toilet that should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (only the dryer as the range hood fan &amp; basement bathroom fan do not vent outside and the main floor bathroom fan was not working), indicated a pressure drop of 1.0 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and hot water tank are chimney vented &amp; would be susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present on the main floor, however did not see any in the basement. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has no exhaust ventilation other than the clothes dryer:

        - the kitchen range hood does not vent out

        - the bathroom fan in the basement does not vent out

        - the bathroom fan on the main floor was not working



You may want to consider replacing both bathroom fans with ENERGY STAR rated fans, vented to the outside of the house, that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.26 per kWh

Oil  - $1.17 per litre 

Propane - $0.69 per litre delivered

Wood - $262.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.34 per bag, $288.68 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high trans</AdditionalComments>
